+All findings below are grounded in this run's events, stage timings, and billing (run 01M256QJB48JK8BXJE1TVM2HYS, seed fabro-afb8, verification-only pass, $0.163 total, 3m31s wall, zero retries). Ordered by expected impact.
+
+## 1. Exit already-landed seeds before the cycle instead of running a no-op verification lap — `planner.md` step 3 + graph
+**What happened:** The fix (commit `7ae575c`, message "…(fabro-afb8)") was already in the run base `3f26ab9`. The planner saw it (event seq 44 reasoning: "the fix appears already implemented!") but the prompt's "looks already implemented → claim verification-only" rule forced the full cycle. The run's entire diff was `.fabro/journal/*.jsonl` + `.seeds/issues.jsonl` (2 files, +8/−1), yet it consumed implementer 59s, tester 4.9s, evidence, reviewer 48s, closeout, and produced PR #115 — a bookkeeping-only PR.
+**Change:** In `planner.md` step 3, make the distinction mechanical: if `git log --grep <seed-id>` shows a fix commit already in base history that references the seed id AND the acceptance criteria hold, close via `sd close --reason "superseded: fix landed in <sha>"` and route exit — reserve the verification-only claim for "criteria look satisfied with *no* referencing commit". Pair with open seed fabro-9f97 (skip PR creation on journal-only diffs).
+**Effect:** Eliminates a whole ~$0.16 / 3.5-min run plus a no-op PR per stale tracker row; with 200 ready seeds, stale rows will recur.
+
+## 2. Cap the `sd ready` firehose — PROJECT_FACTS command table in `planner.md`
+**What happened:** The planner's first call returned **200 seeds / 27,462 bytes** (event seq 32), ballooning conversation tokens 4,967 → 21,113 across turns. Planner = $0.081, **50% of run cost**, for picking the top item of a deterministic list.
+**Change:** First call `sd ready --assignee fabro --limit 10`; widen only when in-flight skips exhaust the window (keeps the fabro-c16d no-silent-truncation guarantee by making widening explicit).
+**Effect:** ~7k tokens out of every planner turn; direct cut to the costliest stage.
+
+## 3. Reviewer: spot-check one criterion on empty-diff verification passes — `reviewer.md` "Your job this pass"
+**What happened:** The evidence header said `seed-work=0 files +0/-0`, yet the reviewer re-ran all four checks the implementer had just run with identical commands (reviewer journal: grep of all three scripts, `nu --ide-check`, `git show 7ae575c`) — 47.9s inference / $0.033 to confirm an empty diff.
+**Change:** Add: "When the capture header shows seed-work=0 and the implementer's PASS report cites its commands, re-run at most ONE criterion as spot-check and judge the rest from the report."
+**Effect:** Roughly halves reviewer wall/cost on trivial passes; aligns with open seed fabro-50c9.
+
+## 4. Acceptance bullets must be literally true as written — `planner.md` steps 6–7
+**What happened:** The brief said "no `get -o` anywhere in smoke.nu", but the planner's own grep (seq 46) had just shown line 89's *comment* containing the literal string. Implementer and reviewer both had to reinterpret ("parse-inert") to PASS; the implementer journaled that any gate grepping `get -o` will false-positive. A stricter reviewer would have routed Changes-requested — a full extra cycle.
+**Change:** One added line: "If your own probe output contradicts a bullet's literal wording, write the exemption into the bullet (e.g. 'no `get -o` in executable code; line-89 comment exempt')."
+**Effect:** Removes an ambiguity that can flip a future review into a ~3-minute re-plan cycle.
+
+## 5. Batch planner reconnaissance — `planner.md` steps 3–4 (open seed fabro-55a7)
+**What happened:** Six sequential shell turns (seq 31, 37+38, 45, 51, 57) over 45s inference; tool time was only 5.6s. `git log --grep <id>` (the decisive stale-basis probe) didn't depend on `sd show` output, and the two grep/sed batches (seq 45, 51) were separable turns.
+**Change:** Prescribe folding `git log --grep <seed-id> --oneline` into the `sd show` batch, and the audit greps into one call.
+**Effect:** 2–3 fewer LLM turns; each turn re-sends ~30k tokens, so this compounds with rec 2.
+
+## 6. Scope memory and PROJECT_FACTS per node — reviewer prompt include + AGENTS.md memory config (fabro-9588)
+**What happened:** All three stages loaded the full 24,605-byte `AGENTS.md` (seq 23, 76, reviewer; memory = 5.9–6.5k tokens, ~20% of each stage's input). The reviewer also received the full tracker *write* command table (`sd update`/`sd close`) it is forbidden to use.
+**Change:** Read-only PROJECT_FACTS subset for `reviewer.md`; per-node memory scoping so the planner doesn't carry implementer-oriented gate/style content.
+**Effect:** ~6k tokens × 3 stages off every turn; smaller attack surface for rogue tracker writes.
+
+## 7. Surface no-op runs in UX — `closeout.nu` / PR postlude + notifications settings
+**What happened:** PR #115's content is journal + tracker flip only; Slack `run.completed` makes it indistinguishable from a code PR.
+**Change:** When the run diff touches only `.fabro/journal/**` and `.seeds/**`, tag the PR title/body "bookkeeping-only" (or skip the PR per fabro-9f97) and note it in the Slack payload.
+**Effect:** Human review queue stops receiving no-op PRs.
